  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "section-1",
      "title": "What is photosynthesis?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b1",
          "object": "heading",
          "position": 0,
          "content": {
            "level": 2,
            "text": "What is photosynthesis?",
            "number": "1"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b2",
          "object": "prose",
          "intent": "orient",
          "position": 1,
          "content": {
            "paragraphs": [
              "A green leaf can make food from sunlight, air, and water. That process is called photosynthesis. Without it, almost every food chain on Earth would collapse."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b3",
          "object": "aside",
          "intent": "warn",
          "position": 2,
          "content": {
            "label": "Watch out",
            "body": "Photosynthesis is not the same as respiration. Plants do both."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b4",
          "object": "prose",
          "intent": "define",
          "position": 3,
          "content": {
            "paragraphs": [
              "Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants use light energy to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b5",
          "object": "list",
          "intent": "name-parts",
          "position": 4,
          "content": {
            "style": "unordered",
            "lead_in": "You will need to know these inputs and outputs:",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "Inputs: carbon dioxide, water, light energy"
              },
              {
                "text": "Outputs: glucose, oxygen"
              },
              {
                "text": "Where: mainly in chloroplasts inside leaf cells"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b6",
          "object": "figure",
          "intent": "show-structure",
          "position": 5,
          "layout": {
            "placement": "spanning"
          },
          "content": {
            "alt_text": "Simple leaf cross-section showing chloroplasts in palisade cells",
            "caption": "Figure 1. Chloroplasts are concentrated where light is strongest.",
            "width": "span",
            "asset": {
              "kind": "svg",
              "status": "ready",
              "svg": "<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 160' role='img'><rect width='400' height='160' fill='#f7f7f7' stroke='#777'/><text x='20' y='40' font-size='14'>Leaf cross-section (placeholder)</text><ellipse cx='200' cy='90' rx='90' ry='35' fill='#cfe8cf' stroke='#333'/><text x='155' y='95' font-size='12'>chloroplast zone</text></svg>"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "section-2",
      "title": "Following the flow",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b7",
          "object": "heading",
          "position": 0,
          "content": {
            "level": 2,
            "text": "Following the flow",
            "number": "2"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b8",
          "object": "prose",
          "intent": "explain-cause",
          "position": 1,
          "content": {
            "paragraphs": [
              "Light energy is captured by chlorophyll. That energy drives reactions that split water and fix carbon dioxide into sugar. Oxygen is released as a by-product."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b9",
          "object": "table",
          "intent": "compare",
          "position": 2,
          "layout": {
            "placement": "spanning"
          },
          "content": {
            "presentation": "comparison",
            "caption": "Word and symbol equations",
            "columns": [
              {
                "id": "form",
                "label": "Form"
              },
              {
                "id": "equation",
                "label": "Equation"
              }
            ],
            "rows": [
              {
                "cells": {
                  "form": "Word",
                  "equation": "carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen"
                }
              },
              {
                "cells": {
                  "form": "Symbol",
                  "equation": "6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂"
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b10",
          "object": "worked-example",
          "intent": "demonstrate",
          "position": 3,
          "content": {
            "title": "Balancing check",
            "problem": "Show that atoms balance on both sides of the symbol equation.",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "Count carbon: 6 on the left, 6 inside glucose on the right."
              },
              {
                "text": "Count hydrogen: 12 from water on the left, 12 in glucose on the right."
              },
              {
                "text": "Count oxygen: 18 on the left, 6 in glucose + 12 in oxygen gas on the right."
              }
            ],
            "answer": "Atoms balance; the equation is conserved.",
            "check": "If oxygen counts do not match, re-check the coefficients."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b11",
          "object": "aside",
          "intent": "memory-aid",
          "position": 4,
          "content": {
            "label": "Memory aid",
            "body": "CO₂ in, O₂ out — plants take the carbon, release the oxygen."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "section-3",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "b12",
          "object": "heading",
          "position": 0,
          "content": {
            "level": 2,
            "text": "Check your understanding",
            "number": "3"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b13",
          "object": "choices",
          "intent": "diagnose-misconception",
          "position": 1,
          "content": {
            "stem": "Which statement is correct?",
            "marks": 1,
            "options": [
              {
                "letter": "A",
                "text": "Plants only photosynthesise; they never respire."
              },
              {
                "letter": "B",
                "text": "Photosynthesis stores light energy as chemical energy in glucose."
              },
              {
                "letter": "C",
                "text": "Oxygen is an essential input for photosynthesis."
              },
              {
                "letter": "D",
                "text": "Photosynthesis happens only at night."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b14",
          "object": "questions",
          "intent": "practise-guided",
          "position": 2,
          "content": {
            "instructions": "Answer in full sentences.",
            "items": [
              {
                "id": "q1",
                "prompt": "Name the two raw materials that enter the leaf for photosynthesis.",
                "marks": 2,
                "answer_lines": 2
              },
              {
                "id": "q2",
                "prompt": "Explain why a plant in continuous darkness will eventually die even if watered.",
                "marks": 3,
                "answer_lines": 4
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b15",
          "object": "list",
          "intent": "summarise",
          "position": 3,
          "content": {
            "style": "ordered",
            "lead_in": "Summary",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy."
              },
              {
                "text": "Inputs are carbon dioxide and water; outputs are glucose and oxygen."
              },
              {
                "text": "Chloroplasts are the sites of the process."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "b16",
          "object": "prose",
          "intent": "connect-forward",
          "position": 4,
          "content": {
            "paragraphs": [
              "Next lesson: how glucose is used in respiration and growth."
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
